From e60bcdef32f1e14d3ef70fa0dcdd1307aabb3c8a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chromium Extend <noreply@localhost>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 10:29:58 +0000
Subject: Offer a download for video the page streams rather than links to

A site that plays video through Media Source gives the context menu nothing to
work with. The <video> element points at a blob, which has no file behind it,
and the real files were fetched by script and never appear as anyone's src. So
long pressing a reel or a story offers no download at all, while the same page
on a desktop browser does.

The files themselves are ordinary and complete. They are fetched over plain
HTTPS, they come back with a real Content-Length, and they can be fetched again
on their own terms. Nothing needs to be broken open to reach them; they only
need to be remembered.

BareMediaRegistry does that remembering. It is a WebContentsObserver that reads
ResourceLoadComplete, which is a report of what the network stack has already
finished doing, and writes down media it sees. It is deliberately passive: no
URLLoaderThrottle, no request rewriting, no interception, no second HTTP stack.
It cannot affect a single byte of what the page loads, because by the time it
hears about a load that load is over.

What gets remembered is narrow. HTTPS and HTTP only, so blob: and data: are
out. GET only. Successful loads only, and only from the primary page, so
prerendered and cached pages do not contribute. Scripts, styles, fonts,
documents, frames and workers are refused whatever content type they carry.
What is left has to look like media either by request destination or by content
type; the second test is the one that matters, because a fetch driven by script
has no destination to go on. Size floors then drop tracking pixels and
interface sounds. Range requests report completion once per range, so repeats
of a URL collapse into one entry holding the largest size seen. The list is
capped at 64, oldest evicted, and cleared whenever the primary page changes.

The menu item appears only where upstream has nothing to offer: a video element
whose src is not downloadable, on a tab that has actually fetched a video. It
downloads the largest one, which for adaptive streaming is the best quality
rendition. The download goes out through ContextMenuItemDelegate and the native
delegate, the same two calls every other download item makes, so the policy
check and the external download manager handoff apply unchanged.

The item is offered when the ordinary one cannot be, and the test for that is
the ordinary item's own condition negated rather than a guess about schemes.
The guess would have been wrong: blob counts as a downloadable scheme, so a
scheme check matches every Media Source video and the item would never have
appeared at all. Blink is the one that knows, and it clears the save flag for
Media Source, so canSaveMedia is the honest signal. Written this way the two
items can never both appear and never both be missing.

Some placement notes. The registry compiles into the context_menu_helper target
because the menu is its only reader, which avoids a new target and a new edge in
chrome/browser/BUILD.gn. It is created next to ContextMenuHelper in
AttachTabHelpers for the same reason. SAVE_DETECTED_VIDEO is appended to the end
of the Item enum, since the id arrays are indexed by those values. No new string
was added: IDS_CONTEXTMENU_SAVE_VIDEO already reads "Download video".

Audio-only streams and separate audio tracks are recognised by the registry but
not yet offered, and a video-only DASH track will download without its sound.
Pairing and remuxing come next.
